{
  "description": "Cross-language behaviour parity: inputs paired with the outcome both implementations must produce. Not values — decisions.",
  "why": "test-vectors/parity.json pins what the two languages compute. This pins what they *do*. The distinction is not academic: both languages produced byte-identical EIP-712 digests for an authorization expiring in the year 2100, agreed on every digest and fingerprint in parity.json, and then TypeScript refused it while Python honoured it. No value vector could have seen that, because no value differed.",
  "authorship": "Hand-written as a specification, deliberately NOT emitted from either implementation. parity.json is emitted from TypeScript and its own header records what that cost: for a while only Python asserted against it, which made TypeScript an oracle, and an oracle cannot be wrong by construction. A behaviour table generated from one side would encode that side's bugs as the contract. Where these cases and an implementation disagree, decide which is right — do not assume it is the code.",
  "reasons": {
    "accept": "The operation is permitted. No error.",
    "AuthorizationExpired": "now > expiry.",
    "AuthorizationLifetimeTooLong": "expiry is further ahead than MAX_AUTHORIZATION_LIFETIME_SECONDS (3600).",
    "AuthorizationMismatch:<field>": "The named field does not describe the situation the authorization was presented in."
  },
  "ordering": "Cases where more than one rule would fire pin *which* refusal wins. Ordering is behaviour: a language that checks the fields before the clock refuses a stale authorization with a mismatch error, which sends whoever reads the log looking for the wrong bug. Expiry, then lifetime, then fields in declaration order.",
  "constants": {
    "maxAuthorizationLifetimeSeconds": 3600
  },
  "migration": [
    {
      "name": "a well-formed authorization for this instance is accepted",
      "now": 1000,
